{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01AMUS6tXLVF4R89aD9UJHeC","type":"tool_result","content":"=== the two source changes in translation/src/lib.rs ===\n167:fn quiet_nan(x: f32) -> f32 {\n168-    f32::from_bits(x.to_bits() | 0x0040_0000)\n169-}\n170-\n171-/// x86 SSE scalar ops select which NaN propagates by *operand position*:\n172-/// `op dst, src` yields `quiet(dst)` when `dst` is NaN, else `quiet(src)` when\n173-/// `src` is NaN.  gcc's register allocation therefore fixes which NaN a\n174-/// mixed-NaN expression returns; these helpers let the translation name that\n175-/// choice explicitly.  For non-NaN operands they are exactly the plain\n176-/// IEEE-754 operation.\n177-#[inline(always)]\n178-fn x86_mul(dst: f32, src: f32) -> f32 {\n179-    let r = if dst.is_nan() {\n...\n180:        quiet_nan(dst)\n182:        quiet_nan(src)\n192:        quiet_nan(dst)\n194:        quiet_nan(src)\n204:        quiet_nan(dst)\n206:        quiet_nan(src)\n218:        quiet_nan(dst)\n220:        quiet_nan(src)\n471:    c2Mulvs(a, x86_div(1.0f32, b))\n\n1437 src/lib.rs\n\n=== Cargo.toml ===\n[package]\nname = \"translation\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\nname = \"omni_manifold_lib\"\npath = \"src/lib.rs\"\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\n\n[profile.release]\nopt-level = 2\npanic = \"abort\"\n\n[workspace]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4bcc327f-132f-463a-99a7-0691637f7d66","uuid":"df56afd6-6026-449b-9aa0-d39618968ca7","timestamp":"2026-08-27T13:32:10.849Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== the two source changes in translation/src/lib.rs ===\n167:fn quiet_nan(x: f32) -> f32 {\n168-    f32::from_bits(x.to_bits() | 0x0040_0000)\n169-}\n170-\n171-/// x86 SSE scalar ops select which NaN propagates by *operand position*:\n172-/// `op dst, src` yields `quiet(dst)` when `dst` is NaN, else `quiet(src)` when\n173-/// `src` is NaN.  gcc's register allocation therefore fixes which NaN a\n174-/// mixed-NaN expression returns; these helpers let the translation name that\n175-/// choice explicitly.  For non-NaN operands they are exactly the plain\n176-/// IEEE-754 operation.\n177-#[inline(always)]\n178-fn x86_mul(dst: f32, src: f32) -> f32 {\n179-    let r = if dst.is_nan() {\n...\n180:        quiet_nan(dst)\n182:        quiet_nan(src)\n192:        quiet_nan(dst)\n194:        quiet_nan(src)\n204:        quiet_nan(dst)\n206:        quiet_nan(src)\n218:        quiet_nan(dst)\n220:        quiet_nan(src)\n471:    c2Mulvs(a, x86_div(1.0f32, b))\n\n1437 src/lib.rs\n\n=== Cargo.toml ===\n[package]\nname = \"translation\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\nname = \"omni_manifold_lib\"\npath = \"src/lib.rs\"\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\n\n[profile.release]\nopt-level = 2\npanic = \"abort\"\n\n[workspace]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_kcd735mw5rrayis2e3sbnro6vfvmfodsdjt3oaf3yejjl6dn6vcq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What I did\n\n**Setup.** `cargo check` was already clean. Built the C `.so` with CMake, added `libloading = \"0.8\"` to `[dev-dependencies]`, and wrote 10 integration test files (133 tests) that `dlopen` **both** `.so`s and call every function through its exported symbol — never a direct Rust call.\n\n**Phase A** — `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (97 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (138 rows), all derived by walking `lib.c` branch by branch. Symbol parity was already exact (46/46, same set, no extras), so no missing C module needed translating.\n\n**Phases B–D** — 2.58 M differential cases, all bit-exact, at Rust opt-levels 0/1/2/3/s/z. `Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]`, so the default build is the only feature configuration (verified via `cargo metadata`); `run_all.sh` enumerates and checks that automatically.\n\n## The real bug I found and fixed\n\n`x86_mul`/`x86_add`/`x86_sub` modelled SSE's operand-position NaN selection but returned the chosen operand **verbatim**. Real `mulss`/`addss`/`subss` *quiet* a signalling NaN first. ~16 % of NaN-carrying cases diverged by exactly bit 22 (C `0x7fd0a74a` vs Rust `0x7f90a74a`) across 12 functions. Fixed with `quiet_nan()`; also wired up the then-unused `x86_div` in `c2Div`.\n\n## Three C behaviours that are not functions of their inputs\n\nI verified these against a standalone C program, so the conclusions don't depend on my harness:\n\n1. **`cache->count >= 4` in `c2GJK` crashes the C itself** (SIGSEGV, exit 135) — it writes past the 136-byte `c2Simplex` into the callee's frame. Documented; `count ∈ {1,2,3}` asserted instead.\n2. **`C2_TYPE_POLY` makes `c2GJK` read its `c2Proxy` locals uninitialised** (`c2MakeProxy` has no such case), and this is reachable from the public API via `omni_manifold(AABB, CAPSULE)`. On a pristine stack the bytes are zero, which is exactly what the Rust materialises — a plain C `main()` reproduces the Rust's bytes exactly. My harness had to scrub 16 KiB of stack before each FFI call, cache all `dlsym` lookups, and `dlopen` with **`RTLD_NOW`** (lazy PLT resolution of `malloc@plt` was deep enough to re-dirty the region — worth exactly 1 divergence in 80 000).\n3. **The `iter < 20` cap is unreachable**; max is 4, since the largest proxy has 4 vertices. I assert 0..=4 all occur and 5+ never does, so the claim can't silently rot.\n\n## Two table rows I had derived wrong, and corrected\n\n`c2Clip`'s `sp` cannot reach 4 (the double-push only fires when `sp` is still 0, so it maxes at 2 and never overflows `out[2]`), and `c2GJK`'s bad-cache-index case is well-defined only for `i ∈ [-1,7]`.\n\n## Caveat on confidence\n\nI mutation-tested the suite with 20 injected faults to check it wasn't passing by accident. 18 were caught (7–71 tests each). Two survived — `fneg` → plain `-` in `c2MulrvT` and `c2KeepDeep` — and I confirmed those are genuinely equivalent (Rust's unary `-` lowers to LLVM `fneg`, a pure sign-bit flip) rather than test gaps. One survivor *was* a real gap: `c2Witness`'s `mulss` operand order. Random NaNs collapse onto the default QNaN and hid it, so I added `phase_c_nan_order.rs`, which sweeps pairwise-distinct NaN payloads across every operand slot (15 new rows), and confirmed the C's choice from `objdump`. Anything below that level of scrutiny — e.g. a wrong operand position that only shows up with two distinct NaN payloads in a path I didn't enumerate — could still be lurking.\n\n`c_src/` is untouched (md5 of `lib.c` unchanged)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1038,"cache_read_input_tokens":394592,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1038,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4bcc327f-132f-463a-99a7-0691637f7d66","uuid":"9efb6a34-9ded-48f6-91b5-380a6eb0491e","timestamp":"2026-08-27T13:32:34.279Z"}
